WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:05.830
SPEAKER: Hello.

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:09.730
This course covers the key steps
to adopting Agile development

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:11.710
in your API program.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:13.870
From our combined
experience at Google,

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:17.830
the first start to any
adoption is a mindset shift.

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:20.360
Agile software development
just makes sense.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.390
And if you've been through
traditional waterfall

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:26.620
development, you know about the
big handovers, big team silos,

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:30.700
and difficulty in
collaborating, and plan change.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:34.330
The values and principles
espoused in this manifesto

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:38.200
will derive and underpin a broad
range of software development

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.990
frameworks, but
primarily seek to solve

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:45.040
the root causes of the software
development in waterfall.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:48.610
The term Agile was
popularized by the Manifesto

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:50.950
for Agile Software Development.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:54.430
Take a moment to read through
it and visit their website.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:57.550
Agile can be adopted in many
different software settings

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:01.040
within the digital value
chain for our course.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.330
We focus on the Agile
method of developing APIs

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:05.860
by the API team.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:11.300
A core part of Agile is to have
one cross-functional API team.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.060
This means the
API team should be

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:17.930
largely self-sufficient with as
few dependencies as possible.

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:21.460
Here's what the Agile
API team would look like.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:25.540
The core API team is in the
middle with one team made up

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:30.790
of the API product owner,
API developers, QA engineers,

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:33.940
and SCRUM master
in the inner ring.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.820
They are constantly
working together.

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:40.270
The outer ring represents
also core team members

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:43.150
who may not be fully
dedicated to the API team,

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:45.280
and might be partially
allocated over time

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.980
to support multiple API teams.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:51.010
The role of connecting
with non-core team members

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:52.810
are represented
with arrows showing

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:55.540
specific individuals who
should take ownership

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:57.520
of these key activities.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.640
We explore each one of the
roles and relationships in depth

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:01.840
throughout this course.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:03.790
For teams starting
out with Agile,

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:07.660
one of the biggest pitfalls
is taking on too much scope.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.090
A great way to adapt
to changing plans

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:14.380
is to reduce the size of each
software scope implement.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.870
Developing software
in a new way can

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:19.550
be difficult for
inexperienced teams.

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:22.600
So a smaller scope is
even more ideal for them.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.300
Size the scope of
your API project right

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:29.020
and focus on the
minimum viable product.

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.510
Ask your team, what
is the least we

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:35.080
need to build that will
still deliver business value?

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:37.210
The picture here shows
three different ways

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:40.630
to build a transport
device over four releases

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:42.820
with a business goal
of getting from point A

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:47.000
to point B. The first
example should be avoided,

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.760
which is building incrementally.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.490
After each release,
there's no business value.

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:53.770
Since we only get
from point A to B

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:56.590
until the very end
in release four.

00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:58.360
The second example is better.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:02.440
It achieves the goal of getting
from A to B in each release

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:03.970
with a working device.

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:07.990
But it forces the team to rework
the product in every release,

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:11.140
leading to a lot of waste
and throw away effort.

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:14.470
The third example
is the MVP approach.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:17.920
We can transport from
A to B in each release.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:19.930
But we have minimal
rework since we're

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:22.660
built on top of the previous
releases functionality

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.400
in a smart sequence.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:28.090
You can think of Agile API
development in just this way.

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:32.290
Deliver the right MVP and pay
close attention to the sequence

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:35.110
you are releasing your new APIs.

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:38.950
To be really efficient, it's
not enough to release often

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:42.130
but to release with
quality and with the least

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:43.630
amount of effort.

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:46.930
Instead of writing test cases
after development is complete,

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:50.950
write the test cases and define
the success criteria upfront

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:54.190
in design before the
development starts.

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:57.370
This practice comes
in two flavors,

00:03:57.370 --> 00:04:02.170
test-driven development, and
behavior driven development.

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:05.470
Both are very similar and
achieve the same outcome.

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:07.960
Both methods output
code that can then

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:11.140
be automated by QA engineers
who can string together

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:16.510
a series of unit tests and
tests written through TDD or BDD

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:20.620
to becoming integration
level tests for the API.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:23.980
By running test cases that
are programmable scripts,

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:27.190
we can run the tests
over and over again,

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:30.640
taking a huge load off the
traditional QA bottleneck

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:35.650
and makes turnarounds on the QA
feedback loop incredibly fast.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:38.080
For more information
about testing,

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:42.190
refer to the QA strategy
course later on in this series.

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:44.770
The principle of
automation is quality--

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:47.890
is an essential part of
Agile transformation.

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:49.810
No other practice
represents this

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:52.840
better than setting up
a continuous integration

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:55.900
and continuous
deployment pipeline.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:59.600
This shortened acronym is CI/CD.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.440
The main goal is to prevent
integration hell, where

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:04.660
multiple developers
begin integrating

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:08.560
large chunks of code near the
later phases of the project.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:11.020
To avoid this drama,
the picture to the left

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:13.990
illustrates a recurring
cycle from code check

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:17.530
in, to testing, to
deployment, of the API bundle,

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:20.320
to updating the
API documentation.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:23.530
The purpose to do this cycle
on a frequent basis or even

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:26.050
on a per code commit basis.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:29.290
By using software like
Jenkins and programming

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:31.510
to move code from each
one of these steps,

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:34.210
this process can be
largely automated.

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:37.300
This reduces human effort
and increases the frequency

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:40.240
that the cycles run so that
errors are caught early

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:42.580
starting from the
first week of coding.

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:45.400
This practice of mastering
the CI/CD pipeline

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:48.740
is essential in adopting
Agile development.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:51.680
Now that we have a good grasp
of the fundamental strategies

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:55.190
and tactics of adopting
Agile, let's discuss

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:57.410
the process of implementation.

00:05:57.410 --> 00:05:59.960
Here's the familiar
waterfall process,

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:04.160
long release with lengthy
stages and big handover points

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.000
often from one team to
another with risk and unknowns

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.960
unsurfaced until we get
into that particular stage.

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:14.390
You've probably been through
it and have some pretty

00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:16.190
interesting stories to tell.

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:18.170
Teams that move
away from waterfall

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:21.260
have a tendency to
move into SCRUM fall.

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:24.740
It breaks the work into sprints
and starts testing cycles

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:27.200
early on so it
helps the team pivot

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:30.840
and provide early
visibility into progress.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:34.430
However, because testing is
separated as a post sprint

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:37.940
activity and not included
during requirements definition

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:42.320
and sprint, testing and bug
fix time is elongated and long

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:45.080
release cycles
invariably happen.

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:48.260
If you have large scope
that you can't break apart,

00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:50.390
this is still better
than waterfall.

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:54.800
However, with API development,
and most software development,

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:57.560
we can definitely go
further than SCRUM fall.

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:00.470
The first important
tactic to avoid SCRUM fall

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:04.880
is to merge development
and QA into one sprint.

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:08.390
This likely means you will
take on less work in the sprint

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:10.340
to give time for bug fixing.

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:13.760
But with testing and fixing
merged into the sprint,

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:16.310
you've tested and ready
to release functionality

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:17.810
at the end of each sprint.

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:20.390
This gives us the
real ability to pivot.

00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:23.840
Make this your first
goal for the team.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:28.050
Just testing furiously during
development isn't enough.

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:30.410
We need to incorporate the
principle of definition

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:32.090
of quality up front.

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:35.330
If developers are not fully
aware of the test criteria

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:38.120
or QA engineers have to
spend more of their time

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:41.120
defining their test
criteria in the sprint,

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:43.700
precious time will be
wasted in the sprint.

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:47.240
Have the QA engineers
and the developers work

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:49.050
with the requirements
to define the test

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:51.350
criteria ahead of the sprint.

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:53.750
This can be done using
test-driven development

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:56.330
method or the behavior
driven development

00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:58.220
we spoke about earlier.

00:07:58.220 --> 00:08:01.670
Go as far as having the test
scripts coded so that the QA

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:04.460
engineers are just making
minor modifications

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:05.870
in the sprint itself.

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:07.760
With testing
definition brought up

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:10.100
in between requirements
and sprints,

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:13.940
your last step is to
automate the CI/CD pipeline.

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:16.760
Don't shortcut or
give up on laying down

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:20.600
the foundation for automation,
testing, and optimizing

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:21.950
the team's process.

00:08:21.950 --> 00:08:24.680
With time, your team will
come to trust the process

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:28.580
as the testing begins to speed
up development and quality.

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:32.480
Now that we have a higher level
approach down, let's dive a bit

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:34.610
deeper into the Agile process.

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:37.130
There are two common
variant processes

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:40.820
in Agile, SCRUM and Kanban.

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:43.400
Please take a moment to
read the descriptions.

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:46.610
While SCRUM has more
processes with more steps

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:49.520
to take into account,
the cycle of development

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:53.030
is more familiar to many teams
and more friendly for new teams

00:08:53.030 --> 00:08:54.650
being formed.

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:57.080
Kanban, although
easy to pick up,

00:08:57.080 --> 00:09:00.290
relies on work that are
smaller chunks of effort

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:02.840
and less dependent
on other work.

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:05.450
Teams that have been working
well together who can really

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:10.100
self manage in Agile may be
better at picking up Kanban.

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:12.170
For the purpose
of this course, we

00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:15.650
will focus on SCRUM given
its complexity and higher

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:17.870
rates of adoption.

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:20.630
Whichever process you
pick, commit to it.

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:22.910
Don't switch back and
forth until you really

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:26.690
feel like the team has lived and
learned through that process.

00:09:26.690 --> 00:09:29.480
Since the SCRUM process
can be quite complex,

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:33.620
we're going to step through
this process one at a time.

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:37.430
A product owner is the
starting point at step one.

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:39.260
It all begins with a vision.

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:43.340
From this vision, we
develop an API roadmap.

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:46.040
This should be a high level
scope of the components needed

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:48.230
to achieve this vision
and deliver value

00:09:48.230 --> 00:09:50.040
for the organization.

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:52.010
This roadmap can
include different sets

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:57.043
of APIs, business adoption, apps
usage, key compliance efforts,

00:09:57.043 --> 00:09:58.585
infrastructure, and
even improvements

00:09:58.585 --> 00:10:00.980
to the developer portal.

00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:04.160
With a product
roadmap, focus on what

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:06.260
will be the first
valuable release.

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:09.440
Keep in mind the MVP principle.

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:11.900
Now that you have the scope
of your first release,

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:15.320
it should be quickly turned
into a rank list of requirements

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:16.790
in step two.

00:10:16.790 --> 00:10:20.480
These requirements are written
in the form of epics and user

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:21.890
stories.

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:23.900
Often at this
point, the habit is

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:26.870
to go into a lot of
requirements detail.

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:27.950
Don't.

00:10:27.950 --> 00:10:31.310
Instead of going deep, list
all the high level features

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:32.480
and requirements.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:34.610
If you're spending more
than a week on this,

00:10:34.610 --> 00:10:37.160
you're likely going to deep.

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:40.160
Step three is where most of
the recurring action process

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:41.400
happens.

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:44.330
The API team will select the
most important user stories

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:46.970
to work on for
the coming sprint.

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:49.390
The user stories have
to be fully fleshed out

00:10:49.390 --> 00:10:53.020
with business, technical,
and testing details.

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:55.690
The API team will
review each user story,

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:58.900
break down the activities into
tasks needed to build this user

00:10:58.900 --> 00:11:01.720
story, estimate the
effort, and commit

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:05.090
to a set of user stories
for the next sprint.

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:08.470
Each sprint can be one
to four weeks in length.

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:13.060
But we often see teams settle on
two weeks as the happy medium.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:15.070
At the end of the
sprint, the user stories

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:18.160
should be completed
and ready for go live.

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:21.670
Anything not finished will be
carried over to the next sprint

00:11:21.670 --> 00:11:24.730
or place back to
the product backlog.

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:27.550
This sprint cycle
repeats itself again.

00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:30.760
Throughout the sprint cycle,
the SCRUM master is the coach

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:32.530
and tracks the
team's productivity

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:34.630
through burn down
and burn up charts

00:11:34.630 --> 00:11:38.200
to measure the team's progress
in the form of velocity.

00:11:38.200 --> 00:11:40.970
We will cover velocity
later in this course.

00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:43.030
In addition to
tracking progress,

00:11:43.030 --> 00:11:46.390
several ceremonies
are core to SCRUM.

00:11:46.390 --> 00:11:51.220
These key ceremonies are listed
in steps four, five, and six.

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:55.930
In step four, the daily SCRUM
is a daily 30 minute activity

00:11:55.930 --> 00:11:58.660
where each member talks
about the progress they made,

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:01.750
their task for the
day, and any blockers.

00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:05.560
This is the time for the team to
take the pulse of the progress,

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:10.150
and drive action, and ask
for help in step five,

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:13.060
a sprint review, also
known as the sprint demo,

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:15.610
occurs at the end of the sprint.

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:18.160
It's generally meant to
recap what has been built

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:21.490
and provides a moment to
celebrate and to publicize

00:12:21.490 --> 00:12:23.380
the achievements of the team.

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:26.230
In step six, the
sprint retrospective

00:12:26.230 --> 00:12:29.260
is a ceremony also at
the end of the sprint.

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:32.740
The teams comes together to
self reflect on what went well

00:12:32.740 --> 00:12:34.570
and what could be improved.

00:12:34.570 --> 00:12:36.790
Oftentimes, the
best improvements

00:12:36.790 --> 00:12:39.160
come from a team retro.

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:41.320
One area of confusion
we encounter

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:44.680
is what happens in between
the steps from product backlog

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:46.180
to finish work.

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:48.760
The ceremonies are
fairly easy to understand

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:51.850
but the sequence can be a
bit tricky to visualize.

00:12:51.850 --> 00:12:54.430
Let's unpack this a bit more.

00:12:54.430 --> 00:12:57.700
Taking another view
of a sprint by sprint,

00:12:57.700 --> 00:13:01.120
you will see a repeating pattern
where the product backlog

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:05.060
items are refined prior to
the start of development.

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:08.600
This repeating effort is
called backlog refinement.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:11.170
A team process that
is usually established

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:13.120
in the beginning
of the project is

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:15.940
called the definition of ready.

00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:19.060
This checklist is the
agreement within the team

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:21.520
of when a story
is refined enough

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:25.450
for the developers and the QA
engineers to start the sprint.

00:13:25.450 --> 00:13:29.320
Thus, this is a clear target
for even the team members doing

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:32.380
refinement on how far
they need to go to meet

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:35.080
the definition of ready.

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:38.090
Refinement is a
collaborative activity.

00:13:38.090 --> 00:13:41.440
It's very important that the
product owner, API architect,

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:45.070
and QA engineer all focus on
the same set of user stories

00:13:45.070 --> 00:13:48.010
in the priority
order of the backlog.

00:13:48.010 --> 00:13:50.530
After they are done
with refining S1,

00:13:50.530 --> 00:13:54.400
they move on to S2
and refine that set.

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:57.730
This continues until the
backlog is all refined.

00:13:57.730 --> 00:14:00.610
Once a set of user stories
in sprint one is refined,

00:14:00.610 --> 00:14:04.060
sprint planning starts as
either a recurring activity

00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:06.550
before the sprint
in small chunks

00:14:06.550 --> 00:14:10.600
or one larger session at the
first activity of the sprint.

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:13.150
The output of sprint
planning is a full set

00:14:13.150 --> 00:14:15.190
of tasks per user story.

00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:18.400
With this, the team goes to
work on development and test

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:21.010
scripts writing in execution.

00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:24.700
This process continues
iteratively in sprint two,

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:26.780
three, and so forth.

00:14:26.780 --> 00:14:28.750
The team also needs
to set the definition

00:14:28.750 --> 00:14:32.690
of done for a user story at
the start of the project.

00:14:32.690 --> 00:14:35.260
This is the line
for the team to draw

00:14:35.260 --> 00:14:38.710
in terms of the standard
of finish and quality

00:14:38.710 --> 00:14:40.600
that should be adhered to.

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:43.300
This way, there's no
misunderstanding at the end

00:14:43.300 --> 00:14:45.770
of the sprint when a user
story is marked done.

00:14:45.770 --> 00:14:49.660
It's up to the team as a whole
to enforce this standard.

00:14:49.660 --> 00:14:51.910
In the last step,
finished work can

00:14:51.910 --> 00:14:55.090
be queued for product
release and bundled

00:14:55.090 --> 00:14:57.220
in the release per sprint.

00:14:57.220 --> 00:15:01.540
The automated CI/CD pipeline
helps the last leg of process

00:15:01.540 --> 00:15:04.540
quickly flow from
test and approve code

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:06.460
into the main branch.

00:15:06.460 --> 00:15:10.570
We cover a ton of concepts
and strategies in this course.

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:13.840
To summarize, create
cross-functional teams

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:16.990
and close the ties
between IT and business.

00:15:16.990 --> 00:15:19.840
Avoid legacy and
waterfall processes

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:22.060
so you can deliver
a smaller scope

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:24.590
and pivot on a regular basis.

00:15:24.590 --> 00:15:29.320
Always look for ways to automate
and reduce any big handovers.

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:33.250
Use methods like behavior
driven development and concepts

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:35.740
like definition of
ready and definition

00:15:35.740 --> 00:15:38.680
of done to build quality
into the process.

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:43.070
And align the teams on
a shared expectation.

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:43.820
Don't worry.

00:15:43.820 --> 00:15:46.330
You won't get it
perfect on day one.

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:48.870
Use the feedback loops
like the sprint ceremonies

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:51.210
to take feedback and optimize.

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:55.710
Every Agile team will tailor
their process over time.

00:15:55.710 --> 00:16:00.210
If there's one more last
principle of Agile to remember,

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:02.340
this is about
continuous improvement.

00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:05.305
That's your North star.

00:16:05.305 --> 00:16:06.680
I hope this course
has helped you

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:09.350
get a firmer grasp on
transforming into an Agile

00:16:09.350 --> 00:16:11.030
development team.

00:16:11.030 --> 00:16:13.550
If you want more
information, please check out

00:16:13.550 --> 00:16:16.400
the community for
ebooks, articles,

00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:19.210
and other related topics.

